## 6. Repository save & persistence behavior

The service depends on the `OwnerRepository` interface (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java`). `save(Owner)` is declared there (lines 87). Under the `spring-data-jpa` profile the implementation is Spring Data JPA:

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java` (lines 36-37):
```java
@Profile("spring-data-jpa")
public interface SpringDataOwnerRepository extends OwnerRepository, Repository<Owner, Integer> {
```
`save` is inherited from Spring Data (no custom `@Query`); it delegates to `EntityManager.persist`/`merge`. The queries used by the create flow are custom JPQL:
- `findByLastNameIgnoreCase` (lines 63-65): `WHERE LOWER(owner.lastName) = LOWER(:lastName)`
- `findByTelephone` (lines 59-61): `WHERE owner.telephone = :telephone`
- `countByCity` (lines 71-73): `WHERE LOWER(owner.city) = LOWER(:city)`
- `countByRegistrationDate` (lines 75-77): `WHERE owner.registrationDate = :registrationDate`

(Alternate profiles `jpa` / `jdbc` exist under `.../repository/jpa/JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl.java` and `.../repository/jdbc/JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`; only one is active per profile.)

Entity persistence details — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`:
- `@Entity @Table(name = "owners")` (lines 35-36).
- Persisted columns include `address`, `city`, `telephone`, `email`, `registration_date`, `customer_code`, `household_id`, `namesake_count`, and `pets` (`@OneToMany cascade = ALL, mappedBy = "owner", fetch = EAGER`, lines 66-67 — so cascading applies to pets, but none are attached during owner create).
- Entity-level Bean Validation constraints (enforced by Hibernate on flush): `address` `@NotEmpty` (39), `city` `@NotEmpty` (43), `telephone` `@NotEmpty @Pattern("^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$")` (47-48). `firstName`/`lastName` `@NotEmpty` inherited from `Person` (lines 31-37 of Person.java). Id is `@GeneratedValue(strategy = IDENTITY)` in `BaseEntity` (lines 33-34), so the DB assigns the id on insert; `isNew()` returns true when id is null, which is how `save` decides insert vs. update.

Key persistence caveat: because the controller is not `@Transactional`, each service call runs in its own transaction. The household-member re-saves (step 7) commit in separate transactions from the final owner insert (step 14); if a later step (telephone/daily/city limit) throws, those member updates are NOT rolled back.
